## Sanity Check: Throw `Error` vs. Return `null` (For Demos)

| Approach | **Throw Error** (Recommended) | **Return `null`** (Factory Alternative) |
|----------|-------------------------------|-----------------------------------------|
| **Demo fit** | ✅ Teaches real-world failure (try/catch) | ⚠️ Hides issues—user must check `if (car)` |
| **State safety** | ✅ No invalid objects exist | ✅ Also safe, but shifts burden to caller |
| **Beginner-friendliness** | ✅ Familiar ("boom, error!") | ⚠️ Extra `if` checks confuse flow |
| **When to use** | Constructors (fail-fast) | Static factory methods (e.g., `Car.create(...)`) |
| **Example cost** | 2 extra lines (try/catch) | Changes to factory pattern (more refactor) |

**Why throw for your demo?** Constructors *should* throw on bad input—prevents "zombie" objects. `null` is better for optional creation (e.g., `parseFromString(str): Car | null`), but keeps class simple here.

**Quick factory bonus if you want null**:
